hello and welcome to the new world  review a source for everything anime and  manga and today we are back with our  second blind review of one black clover  which will be covering the second dark  which i believe has a name this time so  good on it it's called the dungeon  exploration arc and it was quite an  interesting experience actually the arc  begins in such a basic place with a  whole ton of shonen cliches but by the  end we have this really tightly packed  bit of story where nothing seems wasted  no character no action no dialogue is  extraneous and it builds up to explore  one core idea which is family it was all  really well done for the most part  although i do have  some issues of course but before that  please do let me know if you are  enjoying these videos by clicking the  shiny red subscribe button which will  allow you to receive  maybe regular black clover content and  also shove a like on this here video  because out of your spoilers i probably  won't be looking at comments so those  are the two ways that you can let me  know if you want more of me reviewing  the yelling child but the premise of  this arc is pretty mind-blowingly simple  it's basically we finished the game  tutorial and now we're off all on our  own into our first dungeon hurrah which  is actually a dungeon of pretty  monumental importance because this short  secondary arc is quite complicated quite  quickly because there are actually three  factions interested in this newly  discovered location one of which is the  black bulls featuring aston noel and the  previously unexplored luck but we also  have the golden dawn involved in another  trio with juno and  two others but in addition to that we  also have a faction from a rival country  the diamond  kingdom which present our two main  antagonists and from there it's just a  race to see who can get to the dungeon  treasure first with plenty of conflict  between each of the groups it's a very  straightforward story that mostly  derives its depth from character  interactions and flashbacks lots and  lots of flashbacks and i want to begin  with yuno actually he disappeared very  early on in the first dark and i wasn't  quite expecting to see him again so soon  but it was a very pleasant surprise for  the main rival character dude he's  extraordinarily likable and i think i  said something along these lines in the  last video but the quiet cool looking  super talented teenager is very rarely a  character that is meant to be  immediately likable usually they're  incredibly arrogant need to have some  sort of sense smacked into them quite  physically by some punching or something  but probably one of my favorite moments  of this arc was when yuna was talking to  klaus and even issuing him with a  warning not to underestimate astor  which is nothing new he did do similar  things in the previous arc but it's  still satisfying to see the undeniably  most talented person present going no  you don't understand make sure to watch  out for that protagonist i like the  feeling of respect  there plus seeing you know's wind magic  is always a pleasure of all of the  brands introduced so far i think i like  this one the best there's just something  so beautifully sharp about how the wind  magic is drawn like it's all innate but  it also exudes power unlike say the  plant magic which is very ornate but  also very docile or the lightning magic  which was powerful but not  hugely visually appealing to me and just  while we're on the topic of magic this  arc gave me what i was desperately  craving from the previous which was a  vague explanation of the magic system in  black clover it confirmed that mages  require mana which  yeah sure it might seem like an  incredibly obvious thing to say but i  don't recall it being mentioned at all  previously so it's just good to confirm  that rather than assume the thing that  excites me most though is this diagram  that shows us all of the different magic  possibilities which are based on the  four elements and even mixing them  together like how for example missed  magic sits in between water and wind i  live for stuff like this when it comes  to magic i crave rules and structure  because it's so easy to get lost in  poorly created systems i also really  like that a decision was made not to  reveal every magic type immediately  there's a lot of question marks on this  diagram and it makes me want to keep  reading to discover what they all are it  creates nice intrigue and also you need  the structure of rules in order to  enhance the drama when you decide to  break said rules which actually happens  in this very arc with our main  antagonist  mars and may i just say that both mars  and lotus are roughly  1 billion times better than the  antagonist of the last arc who i still  only know as  ice magic man because i was never given  a reason to care about him whatsoever  mars and lotus are very different  starting with lotus he has a captivating  personality he's outwardly casual but  also wildly intelligent and highly  determined he also has the perfect magic  finity being smoked which suits his  character really well because i felt  like i could never get a solid grasp of  lotus he just seemed to put up shroud  after shroud and i didn't really feel  like i knew who he was until the end of  the arc whereas almost every other  character we've met in the series thus  far has given me a strong first  impression that hasn't been subverted as  of yet what i really liked was seeing  lotus's little character info page and  discovering that his likes were his wife  and daughters in fact this page is  everything because we now know that he  is a dedicated family man and more than  likely just fighting to do what's best  for his family and i guess his kingdom  it makes this conflict significantly  more interesting because he doesn't seem  to be a bad guy in the traditional sense  in fact no one really seems to be a bad  guy in this arc everyone's just doing  what they think is the right thing that  needs to be done  and if anything after learning of the  stories behind our antagonists it really  does make me wonder if the clover  kingdom might be the real bad guys  overall then again i don't know because  there was some pretty sinister crap  going on with mars someone who i was  scared of being another stoic  characterless existence when he first  appeared but i was quite pleasantly  surprised  and he's also obviously who i was  referring to when i said the rules of  magic were introduced to be broken  as it is established that mages can only  generally have one affinity under their  command but  mars wields two mineral magic and fire  magic which is the result of a tragic  and horrific situation that i like to  call the azumi dilemma because that's  where i first experienced it and azumi  is basically a film with the premise of  raising a group of assassins since  childhood and then having their final  test before graduating being to kill  their best friend in the class  which i guess yes sorry spoilers for  like the first five minutes of azomi but  whatever it's not a new idea and i'm  sure that it was done long before azumi  but it's always effective and it's quite  heart-wrenching to read especially  because they play up the betrayal of  fans who immediately makes to kill mars  but then we reveal that she only did  that because she knew mars would not be  able to kill  her otherwise and it's just a mess of  raw emotion a really good mess that led  to him inheriting fan's magical ability  through i don't know vaguely defined  wizard science  so as cold and driven as mars is this  added some much needed warmth for the  character  and when we did see his split grimoire  which was very cool by the way it gave  me a good overall picture of who this  guy is and now i'm very excited to see  both mars and lotus in the future  because they're almost like protagonists  to me now in fact i'm kind of more  interested in their journey right now  than i am asters but once again that's  what i love about this arc there were no  bad guys strictly speaking no enemy of  the weak to bring down no evil for the  sake of evil guys showing up this was  all just pure character conflict it's  basically what happens when you take i  don't know how many people were in this  like eight protagonists with their own  stories and you put them together with a  common goal to fight over each and every  character in this arc is profoundly  likable by the end even klaus i should  say who started off as quiet the pompous  dick and then eventually profusely  apologized to both astor and yuno for  looking down on them that was a lovely  moment and as a result i want to see  more of klaus now and the broad idea  that connects all of these characters  with the possible exception of class i  guess  is family at the center of this family  idea are of course  astor and yuno who we have a brief  flashback of coming to the decision that  because neither of them had a family to  call their own then they would just  treat all of their friends as family  starting with each other which you know  that's nice but that also makes these to  the solar core that binds and enlightens  everyone else meanwhile noelle has a  fairly obvious family issue at hand  because we encounter mimosa who sounds  delicious and they have a bit of a noble  tiff before coming together at the end  as mentioned before lotus is revealed to  be a family man and by the end of the  arc mars has even made the first step  towards building that potential familial  connection with lotus but also the mars  and fauna story very much mirrors the  you know  aster situation two parentless children  brought together albeit in a much more  tragic manner and there is another super  obvious exploration of family as well  with luck and i've held off  talking about him because i think this  was by far the least satisfying part of  the arc for me  i do like luck quite a bit he has this  whimsical battle hungry nature which  gives me some serious nefapito from  hunter hunter vibes in fact there's even  a panel where he gives off those same  sort of pito crazy eyes but he has a  very hastily put together flashback  about his mother not appreciating him  until she discovered his one talent  which is fighting  so for reasons she incepts him with the  idea that he needs to keep fighting and  winning which is an interesting idea  actually this deeply rooted desire to  keep fighting into eternity simply to  earn the approval of a parent there's  really quite a lot you could do with  that however  in this arc lock's childhood trauma and  established way of life is flipped on  its head within like  two pages like it goes from luck  thinking to himself i have to keep  fighting and winning or else my mother  won't accept me and if i don't have my  mother then i am completely alone and  then like a page later he sees astor and  goes well you know except for that guy  he's my family now  and to me it feels extraordinary rushed  like i get that asta has his dramatic  semi-speech about luck being his friend  but even that seemed a bit rushed i mean  you met this guy like what a handful of  days ago maybe i think this might have  worked if it wasn't aster who led to  lock's realization like if it had been  another member of the black bulls whom  luck had been with for a much longer  period of time because in the end i just  have a really  difficult time buying that the screams  of a 15 year old child that you met a  couple of days ago can immediately break  through a lifetime of deeply rooted  psychosis but then again it is a shown  in series and not everything can be a  deep exploration  in the end though uh whatever luck story  is still fitting quite nicely with the  oval theme of the ark so it's forgivable  in regards to some important story  related  things though let's get back to astor  and yuno something that really intrigued  me at the beginning of this arc was the  idea of seeing this treasure hunt as  sort of a test race for the ultimate  goal of becoming the wizard king  something low-key with minimal stakes  but representative of a larger journey  to come  and i may and probably i'm just talking  complete crap here but i can see this  small arc evolving into a grander  representation astor and yuno go their  separate ways again  fight for newfound family friends and i  don't know they'll need to eventually  come together again to face some sort of  great opponent on a more  apocalyptic scale before being left to  decide amongst themselves which one will  become the wizard king in a similar  manner in which they needed to decide  which of them would get the dungeon  treasure which turned out to be both  yay oh and actually the treasure itself  was very very important by the way  because both astor and yuno seem to have  received their own individual upgrades  you know seems to have acquired some  sort of  insanely powerful scroll fairy question  mark which i guess now more or less  mirrors asda's bird familiar who has a  name now  it's nero i like it all hail nero but  astor also  appears to receive some sort of upgrade  in the form of a different sword which  very notably saw him  use magic for the first time i think and  i don't quite know how to feel about  this i guess it's heavily implied that  he borrowed the magic from noel and it  hasn't been elaborated on yet but  still i was very very keen on keeping  astor as a pure anti-magic existence so  we might be entering some very  shaky territory now where the power of  friendship allows astor some sort of  access to the magic of his comrades that  he's met  along the way which i don't know look i  should reserve judgement until there's a  solid explanation but the whole thing  made me feel a bit iffy  as did our brief glimpse of golden dawn  leader man if he's not some sort of  grand enemy then well done  tabitha for putting up this good front  because he's just so  damn nefarious like when he's thinking  about yuno and going yes we need him to  grow stronger  and it's just one of those things that  reinforces my previous thought about the  real bad guys or maybe they're not the  neighboring nations and maybe  it's the pinnacle of the clover kingdom  itself and it's a bit harder to tell in  regards to the wizard king who has now  taken a much more active interest in  astor which is  very quick it's chapter 11 and already  senpai has noticed astor which is  something else i don't necessarily like  but it is still too early to judge all  in all though this arc was pretty  surprisingly great after the first  chapter or two i thought it would be a  stock standard action piece that was  designed to do very little but provide  fights and harp on about friendship in  the way that shannon often does but it  accomplished a fairly incredible amount  actually it introduced and built  five new characters all of which i am  very keen to follow which is not an easy  task to accomplish in 11 chapters and i  suppose as a result at least one of  those characters does feel quite rushed  to me obviously being locked and maybe  mimosa i didn't talk much about her but  there wasn't that much to say to be  honest but the others were given quite a  perfect amount of time and space to  develop and overall it was just  really good super tight and i think i'm  looking forward to reading more but that  pretty much does it for this blind  review of the dungeon exploration arc  and what do you guys think please do  leave your thoughts in the comments  below or even join my discord server and  if you're keen for small black clover  content then please do go and check out  some of my other videos or even  subscribe to the channel for very maybe  regular black clover goodness delivered  straight into your youtube feed but for  now this has been the new world review  and i'll see you next time

